detail.abstract:
Large language model (LLM) agents can now edit repositories, search code, and run tests, but their performance on long-horizon coding tasks remains limited. This paper revisits repository-scale bug fixing through a process-aware lens: rather than scoring only whether tests pass, we measure whether an agent discovers the critical evidence (needles) required to solve a task and how quickly it does so.&nbsp;
<div>
 We introduce a formal framework that models tool-using software agents as sequential decision makers operating under explicit budgets (tool calls, wall-clock time). Within this framework, we define an annotated set of needles per task - minimal repository artifacts (files, symbols, configuration flags, or test contracts) that (i) are required to form a correct fix and (ii) are typically under-specified in failing-test descriptions.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Empirically, across 122 long-horizon tasks drawn from six medium-to-large proprietary repositories (2.8k–28.1k files; 0.8–9.3M LOC), five state-of-the-art LLMs show high needle discovery rates (70–83%) but substantially lower patch success (25–35%) within a 40-tool-call, 20-minute budget. We demonstrate that success factorizes into a discovery stage (finding needles) and a synthesis stage (turning discovered evidence into a correct, non-overfitting patch). While discovery degrades with repository distance and issue-description mismatch, synthesis remains the dominant bottleneck even after needles are found. To support more scientifically grounded progress, we contribute: (1) a rigorous formalization of needle discovery, time-to-needle, and missed-needle failure as survival and decomposition metrics; (2) an annotation protocol and taxonomy of needles that captures hidden invariants, cross-module dependencies, configuration switches, utility edge cases, and implicit test/API contracts; (3) an ablation suite isolating search, early testing, file-access constraints, and description noise; and (4) design implications and concrete algorithms for exploration, hypothesis management, and verification.&nbsp;
</div>

Traditional macroeconomic models have demonstrated significant explanatory poverty in accounting for the asymmetrical global volatility of 2025-2026. This paper proposes a novel paradigm: "Pathological Economics." By utilizing NLP clustering on global semantic heatmaps (China, USA, France, Iran) and integrating Freudian clinical psychology, we demonstrate that global economic growth is essentially the intensity of individual "Libidinal Investment" into external objects.&nbsp;
<div>
 Key Findings:&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 1. China is in a state of "Depressive Stupor" characterized by libidinal withdrawal due to Superego over-censorship.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 2. The United States is in a stage of "Schizoid Mania" driven by death-anxiety, acting as a global "Super-spreader" of cognitive dissonance.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 3. France is experiencing "Acute Withdrawal Agitation" resulting from the interruption of the "All-Powerful Mother" (fiscal welfare) supply.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 4. Iran presents systemic panic attacks against a backdrop of acute organ failure.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The paper concludes that the primary risk of 2026 is not inflation, but an "Immunity Storm" following the collapse of global psychological defense mechanisms.
